MarDRe
------

MarDRe is a de novo MapReduce-based parallel tool to remove duplicate
DNA reads. Duplicate reads can be seen as identical or nearly identical 
sequences with some mismatches. This tool allows bioinformatics to avoid
the analysis of not necessary reads, reducing the time of subsequent 
procedures with the dataset (e.g., assemblies, mappings, etc.).

This tool is distributed as free software and is publicly available under 
the GPLv3 license at http://mardre.des.udc.es.


Quick User's Guide
------------------

- Prerequisites

1) Make sure you have Java Runtime Environment (JRE) version 11 or above

2) Make sure you have a working Hadoop distribution version 3.3.6 (the
   version MarDRe is built and tested with)

  - HADOOP_HOME environmental variable must be set accordingly

3) Untar the downloaded MarDRe distribution

  - On Linux/Mac OS, just follow the instructions below:

    $ tar xzf MarDRe-v2.0.tar.gz

  - Alternatively use your preferred archive extraction tool

4) Set MARDRE_HOME and PATH environmental variables

  - On Linux/Mac OS you can set them in your profile or your shell 
    configuration files (e.g., .bashrc). Follow the instructions below:

    $ export MARDRE_HOME=/path/to/mardre
    $ export PATH=$MARDRE_HOME/bin:$PATH

- Execution:

The following command removes the duplicates of identical reads (no mismatches)
of a single-end dataset using a prefix length of 15 and 8 reducers:

  $ mardrerun -i dataset.fastq -l 15 -nr 8

The following command shows a similar example but for paired-end computing,
allowing two mismatches and using the default prefix length (20) and 8 reducers:

  $ mardrerun -i dataset1.fastq -p dataset2.fastq -m 2 -nr 8

The following command only removes the optical duplicates (duplicate reads
of the same tile that are at most 100 units apart, according to the :X:Y
coordinates of their names) of a single-end dataset:

  $ mardrerun -i dataset.fastq -d 100 -nr 8

The names of the reads are kept as they are in the dataset. Generic Hadoop
options can be given before the options of MarDRe. For instance, the names
can be cut at their first space (but not when using -d) with:

  $ mardrerun -Dhsp.trim.sequence.name=true -i dataset.fastq -nr 8

See the User's Guide (doc/MarDRe-UsersGuide.pdf) and the file CHANGELOG for
the full list of options and the changes of this version.


Building MarDRe from the sources
--------------------------------

The distributed JAR (target/MarDRe-bin.jar) already contains everything that
MarDRe needs but Hadoop. In order to recompile it, make sure you have:

1) Java Development Kit (JDK) version 11 or above

2) Apache Maven version 3 or above

3) The Hadoop Sequence Parser (HSP) library version 2.0.0 installed in your
   Maven local repository. It is not published in any public Maven repository,
   so it is a requirement to install it before building MarDRe:

    $ git clone --branch v2.0.0 https://github.com/UDC-GAC/hsp.git
    $ cd hsp
    $ mvn install

Then, execute the following command from within the MarDRe root directory:

    $ mvn package
